-- Pair: product type (two-element tuple), and the prelude's TWO orderings of it
-- (proposal 058 §3.8, §5; implementation notes §8 phase 7).
--
-- ── EQUALITY IS COMPONENTWISE, AND CONDITIONAL ───────────────────────────────────
--
-- `Pair` provides `PartialEq`/`Eq` for ITSELF by comparing the two components, so the
-- two `requires PartialEq[…]` clauses below do double duty: they CONDITION the
-- provision and they are the EVIDENCE the `eq` body dispatches through. That is the
-- `Set` precedent — `set.anthill`'s sort-level `requires Eq[T]`, honoured through the
-- `provides` fact rather than op-scoped — applied to a two-parameter carrier.
--
-- THE CHAIN IS `PartialEq`, NOT `Eq`, AND THAT IS THE WEAKER-THAN-IDEAL HALF. A sort
-- has ONE `requires` chain, shared by every provision it makes, so the ideal split —
-- `PartialEq[Pair]` conditioned on component `PartialEq`, `Eq[Pair]` conditioned on
-- component `Eq` — is NOT expressible: both provisions ride one chain. `PartialEq` is
-- the right chain for the two things that must hold: it is exactly what the `eq` body
-- needs, and it is what keeps `Pair` a general PRODUCT — with an `Eq` chain,
-- `Pair[A = Float, B = Int64]` became a load error (MEASURED), `Float` providing
-- `NonEq` and WI-835's use-site check refusing it. The residue is that `provides
-- Eq[Pair]` is then slightly too STRONG: it claims lawful equality even where a
-- component only has the partial one. Per-provision conditions (058 §3.8's `provides
-- X[…] :- goals`) are what fix it: WI-869.
--
-- ONE MORE MEASURED LIMIT, and it is not this file's defect: a NESTED pair whose FIRST
-- component is a `Pair` and whose second is a primitive
-- (`eq(pair(fst: pair(fst: 1, snd: 2), snd: 7), …)`) dies at eval, the second
-- component's compare dispatching to `Pair.eq`. That is a PRE-EXISTING defect in how a
-- componentwise provider's dictionary slots are indexed — reproduced on a purely local
-- carrier with no prelude involvement, and `A = Int64, B = <carrier>` passes — but it
-- is this file that makes it reachable from the standard library. WI-871, and pinned in
-- `wi858_pair_orderings_test` so it is not discovered twice.
--
-- ── AND THIS IS WHAT LETS THE TWO ORDERINGS SHIP HERE ────────────────────────────
--
-- `Ordered requires Eq[T]`, so a provider of `Ordered[Pair[…]]` is only accepted once
-- `Eq[Pair[…]]` resolves — and it resolves HERE, in the prelude, from `Pair`'s own
-- provision. That is the whole reason the orderings below can be prelude-owned:
-- `docs/brainstorms/prelude-multiple-orderings.md`'s obstacle B measured that NO
-- primitive's lawfulness is prelude-level (`Eq[String]`, `Ordered[Int64]` and their
-- kin live only in the per-language binding files, proposal 038), so a prelude witness
-- ordering `String` or `Int64` cannot discharge its own `Eq` leg in a binding-free
-- load. A prelude-LAWFUL carrier can carry prelude-owned orderings; a primitive cannot.
enum anthill.prelude.Pair
  import anthill.prelude.{Bool, PartialEq, Eq}

  sort A = ?
  sort B = ?
  requires PartialEq[A]
  requires PartialEq[B]
  entity pair(fst: A, snd: B)

  provides PartialEq[Pair]
  provides Eq[Pair]

  operation fst(p: Pair) -> A
  operation snd(p: Pair) -> B
  -- TODO (WI-324 follow-up): add operation bodies. Both `match p case pair(a, _) -> a`
  -- and `p.fst` currently fail the typer with "expected A, got Term" — the entity-field
  -- sort doesn't propagate through self-typed match/access in concrete enum sorts. The
  -- rules below remain as the de-facto implementation until the typer issue is fixed.
  rule fst(pair(?a, ?b)) <=> ?a
  rule snd(pair(?a, ?b)) <=> ?b

  -- The component compares go through the SEMANTIC spec op `PartialEq.eq`, not through
  -- structural unification, so equality dispatches all the way down — a pair of `Set`s
  -- compares its component by MEMBERSHIP. Each call resolves through the matching
  -- `requires` slot above (`al: A` → the `Eq[A]` slot, `ar: B` → the `Eq[B]` one).
  operation eq(a: Pair, b: Pair) -> Bool =
    match a
      case pair(al, ar) ->
        match b
          case pair(bl, br) ->
            if PartialEq.eq(al, bl) then PartialEq.eq(ar, br) else false
end

-- ── TWO LAWFUL ORDERINGS OF ONE CARRIER, IN THE SHIPPED LIBRARY ──────────────────
--
-- The point of proposal 058 stated as library code: `Pair` has NO canonical order, two
-- lexicographic ones are equally defensible, and both ship. A use site picks with the
-- ordinary call bracket — `SortedSet.empty[T = Pair[Int64, Int64], O = PairByFst]()` —
-- and a bracket-LESS `Ordered.compare` on a `Pair` is a loud tier-3 error naming both.
-- That last part is deliberate and is the reason this pair of witnesses can live in the
-- prelude at all while `ByLength`/`Alphabetical` for `String` cannot: `String` already
-- has an `Ordered` provider, so a prelude rival would turn every downstream
-- bracket-less string compare into an error the consumer never opted into. `Pair` has
-- no provider to displace, so silence was never meaningful here and staying loud costs
-- no one anything. There is deliberately NO `DefaultProvider` row for `(Ordered, Pair)`
-- either — no canonical pair order exists, so tier 3 is the correct answer, not a gap.
--
-- WHY EACH WITNESS IS A BUNDLE (058 §3.8). `ordered.anthill:44-48` DERIVES the
-- inherited `gt`/`lt`/`gte`/`lte` from `compare`, and those come from the carrier's
-- `PartialOrd` via `Ordered requires PartialOrd[T]`. A lone alternative `Ordered`
-- witness over a carrier that already had its own `PartialOrd` would therefore
-- contradict the surface it inherits — for ANY order but the carrier's own. So each
-- witness supplies BOTH floors, mutually consistent, anchored to the one shared `Eq`
-- (which stays outside the bundle: equality is coherent, §3.7). Generalizes to any spec
-- tower — an alternative is a consistent bundle of floors, never one floor over shared
-- lower ones. A compare-`fst`-ONLY witness is additionally a PREORDER: it answers 0 for
-- distinct pairs, and `SortedSet.insertSorted` reads 0 as "already present", so it
-- would silently DROP elements. Do not add one.
--
-- WHY THE ELEMENT SLOTS ARE NAMED. `requires OA: Ordered[A]` is a NAMED slot, hence a
-- type parameter of the witness: which element ordering a pair ordering was built over
-- is part of that ordering's identity, not incidental — the `ListOrd[OE = …]` reasoning
-- of 058 §3.8. The bracket that BINDS one in a value position (`O = PairByFst[OA =
-- Descending]`) is measured NOT to steer the sub-goal yet — WI-870.
sort anthill.prelude.PairByFst
  import anthill.prelude.{Int64, Pair, Ordered, PartialOrd}
  import anthill.prelude.Pair.{pair}
  import anthill.prelude.PartialEq.{eq}

  sort A = ?
  sort B = ?
  requires OA: Ordered[A]
  requires OB: Ordered[B]

  provides PartialOrd[Pair[A, B]]
  provides Ordered[Pair[A, B]]

  -- Lexicographic, `fst` first. The second component decides only on a TIE, so the
  -- only question the first compare's result has to answer is whether it is 0 — a
  -- three-way `lt`/`gt` split would return `c` from two of its arms.
  operation compare(a: Pair[A, B], b: Pair[A, B]) -> Int64 =
    match a
      case pair(al, ar) ->
        match b
          case pair(bl, br) ->
            let c = Ordered.compare(al, bl)
            if eq(c, 0) then Ordered.compare(ar, br) else c
end

sort anthill.prelude.PairBySnd
  import anthill.prelude.{Int64, Pair, Ordered, PartialOrd}
  import anthill.prelude.Pair.{pair}
  import anthill.prelude.PartialEq.{eq}

  sort A = ?
  sort B = ?
  requires OA: Ordered[A]
  requires OB: Ordered[B]

  provides PartialOrd[Pair[A, B]]
  provides Ordered[Pair[A, B]]

  -- The mirror image: lexicographic, `snd` first. Both are total orders agreeing with
  -- `Pair`'s componentwise `Eq` (`compare` is 0 exactly when both components are), so
  -- both satisfy `ordered.anthill`'s `compare_eq` law — which the `fst`-only preorder
  -- above does not.
  operation compare(a: Pair[A, B], b: Pair[A, B]) -> Int64 =
    match a
      case pair(al, ar) ->
        match b
          case pair(bl, br) ->
            let c = Ordered.compare(ar, br)
            if eq(c, 0) then Ordered.compare(al, bl) else c
end
